  "auth_observations": [
    "No Authorization, Cookie, CSRF, or API-key-style auth headers were observed on core Premier League or Pulselive API requests; all appear to be public, unauthenticated GETs.",
    "BlazeSDK requests include an ApiKey query parameter in the URL (public SDK key) rather than a secret Authorization header.",
    "Firebase-related endpoints (installations and remote config) are called without explicit auth headers from the browser; they rely on embedded API keys in the URL and Firebase platform rules.",
    "Analytics and tracking endpoints (TikTok, Spotify, Adobe, Sentry, Google adtrafficquality) similarly do not show explicit Authorization headers; they identify the app via pixel ids or keys in the URL or body.",
    "CORS preflight (OPTIONS) requests are present for several POST endpoints (Blaze audit, Firebase), but they do not carry auth tokens either."
  ],
  "narrative": "346 total requests were captured across 18 hosts. Core business traffic is concentrated on api.premierleague.com (132 requests) and sdp-prem-prod.premier-league-prod.pulselive.com (84 requests), which together account for 216 requests. Static config from resources.premierleague.com contributes 18 requests. Roughly 80 requests are clear analytics/tracking noise (TikTok, Spotify, Adobe, Sentry, Blaze audit, Google adtrafficquality, Firebase infra), and about 32 are other supporting or navigation requests (HTML pages, reCAPTCHA, ECAL, ipapi).\n\nDistinct API patterns were grouped by URL and normalized query parameters. On the data side, Pulselive provides the structured football data: standings, matches, teams, team form, next fixtures, player profiles and stats, and match events/lineups. The most central endpoints for automating league-table and match-related tasks are:\n- /api/v5/competitions/8/seasons/2025/standings (league table)\n- /api/v2/matches and /api/v1/competitions/8/seasons/2025/matchweeks/27/matches (fixtures by competition/season/matchweek or other filters)\n- /api/v1/competitions/8/seasons/2025/teams and /api/v2/teams/1, /api/v2/teams-by-id (club lists and details)\n- /api/v1/competitions/8/seasons/2025/teams/players/stats/leaderboard and /api/v2/competitions/8/seasons/2025/players/{playerId}/stats (player statistics)\n- /api/v1/matches/2562154/events and /api/v3/matches/2562154/lineups (per-match timelines and lineups).\n\napi.premierleague.com complements this with content and media endpoints: generic content search (/content/premierleague/en), playlists, text and video detail endpoints, promo blocks tied to team metadata, and photo endpoints, plus broadcasting schedule and match-events endpoints. These are high-value for automating content discovery and linking to the structured data from Pulselive.\n\nNo explicit authentication is used for these football data/content APIs: they are all simple GET requests over HTTPS with standard browser headers. There are no Authorization or Cookie headers on the core APIs in this capture, implying the data is public-read. Third-party SDKs (Blaze, Firebase, TikTok, Spotify, Adobe, Sentry) use embedded keys or ids in URLs/bodies and do not expose bearer tokens.\n\nFor automation, the main endpoint that returns the league-table data needed for table-related tasks is sdp-prem-prod.premier-league-prod.pulselive.com/api/v5/competitions/8/seasons/2025/standings, which surfaces the current standings for competition 8. Combined with the matches and team endpoints on the same host, and the broadcasting/content endpoints on api.premierleague.com, it is possible to reconstruct tables, fixtures, and rich content around each club or match programmatically. Noise endpoints (analytics pixels, tracking, CORS preflights, reCAPTCHA, adtrafficquality) can be safely ignored when building such automations."
